import ProductionLayout from '../layouts/ProductionLayout.astro';
---
<ProductionLayout title="About — Skyhook Industries" description="About Skyhook Industries, its engineering thesis, founder and operating principles.">
  <section class="page-hero paper-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">About Skyhook Industries</p>
    <h1>Build the part that stays in space.</h1>
    <p>Skyhook Industries is an independent engineering project exploring rotating orbital tethers as reusable transportation infrastructure. The thesis is simple: if part of the launch system can remain in orbit and be used again, each mission does not have to buy the same velocity from scratch.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="paper-section">
    <div class="section-grid">
      <p class="chapter-label">01 / The thesis</p>
      <div>
        <h2>Infrastructure changes the economics of motion.</h2>
        <p class="lede">A rocket carries its propellant, structure and engines through the mission. A Skyhook would move part of the acceleration problem into a persistent orbital machine.</p>
        <p class="body-copy">That does not remove rockets from the system. It changes the job they have to do. Instead of carrying a payload all the way to its final orbital energy, an arriving vehicle would climb to a timed handoff and let the tether perform the next part of the acceleration through momentum exchange.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="dark-section">
    <div class="section-grid">
      <p class="chapter-label">02 / Why this project</p>
      <div>
        <h2>The interesting part starts after the equation works.</h2>
        <p class="body-copy">Rotating tether mechanics have been studied for decades. The harder question is whether the idea can become a maintainable transportation system: one that can be built, deployed, inspected, controlled, repaired, survived through damage, recovered after each payload and operated often enough to matter.</p>
        <p class="body-copy">Skyhook Industries exists to treat that system-level problem seriously, without pretending that a compelling orbital-mechanics diagram is the same thing as an engineered machine.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="paper-band">
    <section class="paper-section">
      <div class="section-grid">
        <p class="chapter-label">03 / Principles</p>
        <div>
          <h2>How the work should be done.</h2>
          <div class="principles">
            <article class="principle"><b>01 / TEST THE ASSUMPTION</b><h3>Build experiments around what could make the design wrong.</h3><p>A good prototype should answer a question. It does not need to resemble the final machine if a simpler article can expose the same uncertainty.</p></article>
            <article class="principle"><b>02 / DESIGN THE FAILURE</b><h3>A broken system is still a system state.</h3><p>Damage tolerance, severed trajectories, aborts and loss of control belong in the architecture from the beginning.</p></article>
            <article class="principle"><b>03 / CLOSE THE WHOLE LOOP</b><h3>Do not optimize one spectacular number.</h3><p>Tip speed means little without tether mass, payload mass, capture loads, orbit loss, recovery power and mission cadence.</p></article>
            <article class="principle"><b>04 / SHOW THE EVIDENCE</b><h3>Separate what flew from what was modeled from what we propose.</h3><p>Credibility comes from clear boundaries, not from making every sentence sound certain.</p></article>
            <article class="principle"><b>05 / KEEP THE AMBITION</b><h3>Rigor is not the same thing as timidity.</h3><p>The purpose of careful engineering is to make large things possible—not to make them sound smaller.</p></article>
            <article class="principle"><b>06 / EARN THE NEXT SCALE</b><h3>Length and cost follow knowledge.</h3><p>Each larger demonstrator should exist because the previous one closed the risks that mattered at its scale.</p></article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="paper-section">
      <div class="section-grid">
        <p class="chapter-label">04 / Founder</p>
        <div>
          <h2>Isaac Mattoo</h2>
          <p class="lede">Founder of Skyhook Industries, with a professional background in complex technical infrastructure and systems engineering.</p>
          <p class="body-copy">The project is intentionally interdisciplinary. A transportation-scale tether touches orbital mechanics, structures, materials, controls, plasma physics, navigation, robotics, manufacturing, debris analysis and operations. Skyhook Industries is not presented as having solved all of those disciplines internally. The goal is to build a coherent system problem that serious specialists can attack together.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>

  <section class="closing">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Work with us</p>
      <h2>Hard problems are better when they are specific.</h2>
      <p>If your work touches tether materials, capture systems, orbital dynamics, electrodynamic propulsion, debris analysis or high-altitude flight, there is probably a useful question here.</p>
      <a class="cta" href="/contact/">Get in touch →</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</ProductionLayout>
